Then, you have some time to look at questions 12 to 16.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:53.000
You will hear a potter, Heather McCallum, speaking to a group of people who are visiting

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:58.000
a place where people make pots and other objects from clay.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Now listen carefully and answer questions 11 to 16.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Hello and welcome. My name's Heather McCallum and I'm one of the potters who work here at

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:27.000
Edelman Pottery. Before we go into the workshop, I just want to say a bit about the craft of

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:35.000
pottery. Then we'll have a look at the equipment and you can try making a pot of your own.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Like many people, I'm sure you know that pottery as an art form is tens of thousands of years

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:51.000
old and we know this because it stands the test of time. Things like baskets and pictures

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:58.000
don't survive in the earth in the same way that pots do and even if ancient pots are

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:05.000
found in small pieces, they still provide a lot of information about the past.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:14.000
There's no doubt that pottery has given archaeologists a fascinating insight into how ancient humans

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:21.000
The shape of an artefact may have been lost, but archaeologists can tell whether the pots

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:29.000
were for, say, storage or cooking by examining the impressions on the clay, the scratches

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:37.000
from tools, and the clay itself can reveal where the pots came from.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:43.000
When I ask people why they want to take a pottery class with me, they sometimes talk

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:50.000
about these things. Like our ancestors, they hope that something they create will also

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:58.000
last longer than they do, that their work, whether it is good or not, might say something

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:03.000
about humanity many years after their death.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:10.000
Of course, you will all have your own reasons for coming here. As far as I'm concerned,

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:17.000
what I love most is the concentration you need to make a good pot. That focus takes

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:24.000
you away from the stresses of everyday life. If you're elderly, it's also good exercise

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:32.000
for hands and wrists and helps with arthritis. And of course, it's a fun activity for children

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:40.000
because it's so messy. Here at Edelman Pottery, we show you some of the basic pottery techniques

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:47.000
so that you can use these to create whatever you wish, a gift for a friend perhaps. Like

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:52.000
nearly everyone who comes here, I'm sure this is the first time you will have tried the

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.000
art, so we'll keep things simple today.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:03.000
Now, before we move on, can I just say a word about what you're wearing? As we said

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:12.000
in our email, please remove any watches, necklaces, etc. and put them somewhere safe. If you have

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:20.000
long hair, do tie it back now. We'll provide aprons later, but I trust your clothes are

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:27.000
old but comfortable, not your favourite t-shirt or jeans.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Before you hear the rest of the talk, you have some time to look at questions 17 to 20.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Now listen and answer questions 17 to 20.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:10.000
So now we're in the workshop. Have a look around. There's a lot going on. To make pottery

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:17.000
that will last, you need a potter's wheel, a kiln, which is basically a very hot oven

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:27.000
where you fire the pottery, and some tools. So first, the kiln. If you look over in the

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:35.000
far corner, you'll see one of ours. Since their invention, kilns have changed very little,

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:42.000
though in the past 20 years, a lot of progress has been made in temperature control. Basically,

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:51.000
a kiln removes the water from clay at temperatures of around 1000 degrees Celsius. This allows

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:59.000
anything you've made to set permanently in shape. It's a pretty ugly, heavy object that's

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:07.000
hard to keep in a house or flat, so most people don't have one. You may think, can't I use

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:15.000
my oven? Well, that's possible, but domestic ovens don't really get hot enough, and eventually

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:24.000
the clay will crack and fall apart. Some people fire pottery in a fire pit outside, but bear

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:32.000
in mind that can be dangerous. You also need to know about safety procedures for kilns

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:41.000
as they release toxic compounds into the air. Every potter needs a potter's wheel.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:49.000
This machine is used to shape the clay into an object with circular walls or sides, such

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:58.000
as a bowl. Its invention revolutionised the pottery industry, allowing multiple items

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:06.000
to be produced in a day. Lastly, there are a number of different tools that potters use,

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:12.000
depending on what they want to make. When you start, your hands can make all kinds of

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:20.000
shapes and curves without relying on a sculpting tool. However, there are some basic tools

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:27.000
that you will need to handle the clay on the wheel. Some look very strange and have

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:34.000
even odder names that you may find hard to remember. Rather than go through them all

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:42.000
now, I'll just name a few tools as we go along. We can provide these, and I wouldn't recommend

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:50.000
spending money on them yet. So, let's try making a pot of your own. If you sit down...

00:07:54.000 --> 00:08:02.000
That is the end of part 2. You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to part 2.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:16.000
Thank you for watching.

